{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"FIN-Seq: transcriptional profiling of specific cell types from frozen archived tissue of the human central nervous system.\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":50,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"31728515","source":"MED","pmid":"31728515","pmcid":"PMC7145626","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC7145626"]},"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkz968","title":"FIN-Seq: transcriptional profiling of specific cell types from frozen archived tissue of the human central nervous system.","authorString":"Amamoto R, Zuccaro E, Curry NC, Khurana S, Chen HH, Cepko CL, Arlotta P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Amamoto R","lastName":"Amamoto","firstName":"Ryoji","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Genetics and Ophthalmology, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Blavatnik Institute, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Zuccaro E","lastName":"Zuccaro","firstName":"Emanuela","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8978-7206"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Curry NC","lastName":"Curry","firstName":"Nathan C","initials":"NC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Khurana S","lastName":"Khurana","firstName":"Sonia","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen HH","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Hsu-Hsin","initials":"HH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Cepko CL","lastName":"Cepko","firstName":"Constance L","initials":"CL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Genetics and Ophthalmology, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Blavatnik Institute, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Arlotta P","lastName":"Arlotta","firstName":"Paola","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA."},{"affiliation":"Stanley Center for Psychiatric Research, Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard, Cambridge, MA 02142, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8978-7206"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"48","journalIssueId":2897870,"dateOfPublication":"2020 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2020,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nucleic Acids Res","title":"Nucleic acids research","essn":"1362-4962","issn":"0305-1048","isoabbreviation":"Nucleic Acids Res","nlmid":"0411011"},"printPublicationDate":"2020-01-01"},"pubYear":"2020","pageInfo":"e4","abstractText":"Thousands of frozen, archived tissue samples from the human central nervous system (CNS) are currently available in brain banks. As recent developments in RNA sequencing technologies are beginning to elucidate the cellular diversity present within the human CNS, it is becoming clear that an understanding of this diversity would greatly benefit from deeper transcriptional analyses. Single cell and single nucleus RNA profiling provide one avenue to decipher this heterogeneity. An alternative, complementary approach is to profile isolated, pre-defined cell types and use methods that can be applied to many archived human tissue samples that have been stored long-term. Here, we developed FIN-Seq (Frozen Immunolabeled Nuclei Sequencing), a method that accomplishes these goals. FIN-Seq uses immunohistochemical isolation of nuclei of specific cell types from frozen human tissue, followed by bulk RNA-Sequencing. We applied this method to frozen postmortem samples of human cerebral cortex and retina and were able to identify transcripts, including low abundance transcripts, in specific cell types.","affiliation":"Department of Stem Cell and Regenerative Biology, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"R01MH09606","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIMH NIH HHS","acronym":"MH","grantId":"R01 MH112940","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIMH NIH HHS","acronym":"MH","grantId":"R01 MH096066","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"R01MH112940","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cerebral Cortex","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Neurons","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CL","qualifierName":"classification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retina","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":